lesson physics-sounds-come-from-shaking v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=7 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Sounds come from shaking"
  goal "Say that a sound is made by something shaking, that a harder shake is a louder sound, and that a shorter band makes a higher note."

  look {
    scene 36x12 {
      row both at=canvas(1, 0) space=2 align=start {
        bands b pluck=1
        ricedrum d hit=2
      }
    }
    say """
      Every sound is made by something shaking. Pluck a band and it shakes so fast it looks
      blurred. Hit a drum and its skin shakes, and the rice on top jumps to show it. The harder you
      hit, the harder it shakes, and the louder the sound.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.band-plucked count=2 seed=1
    practice physics.drum-still count=2 seed=2
    practice physics.drum-harder count=2 seed=3
    practice physics.drum-louder count=2 seed=4
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.band-highest a=12 b=6 c=9
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.drum-order f=1
  }
  remember {
    say "No shaking, no sound. A bigger shake makes a louder sound."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Put a few grains of rice on a pan lid or a drum and tap it gently, then harder. Stretch an
    elastic band round a box and pluck it, then press it down halfway along and pluck the shorter
    part. The high and low question is the first time pitch and loudness come apart: a short band
    is higher, not louder. Children often mix the two words, so use both on purpose.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the two drums compared are hit softly and hard, so the rice shows the difference plainly."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the two drums compared are hit one step apart, so the rice has to be looked at closely."
  }
}
